---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import SquareFootageCalculator from '@components/islands/SquareFootageCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('everyday', 'square-footage-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How do I calculate square footage?', answer: 'Multiply the length by the width of the area in feet. A room 12 ft by 15 ft is 12 × 15 = 180 square feet. Each measurement has its own unit here, so a wall measured in inches and another in feet both work — every dimension is converted to feet before the area is worked out.' },
  { question: 'How do I measure the square footage of a room?', answer: 'Measure the longest length and the widest width with a tape measure or laser measure, rounding to the nearest few inches, then use the Rectangle calculator. Measure at floor level and ignore small alcoves for a quick figure, or add them separately for precision.' },
  { question: 'How do I calculate the square footage of an L-shaped or irregular room?', answer: 'Split the space into rectangles, calculate each one, and add the results. An L-shaped room is two rectangles; a room with a bay is one rectangle plus the bay. For a round, triangular, sloped or wedge-shaped area, use the matching shape below rather than approximating it as a rectangle.' },
  { question: 'What is the Rectangle Border calculator for?', answer: 'It gives the area of the band running around the inside edge of a rectangle — a walkway around a patio, a border of tile around a floor, a mulched strip inside a raised bed. The border is measured inwards from the outer dimensions you enter, so a 30 by 20 area with a 2-wide border is 600 − 26 × 16 = 184.' },
  { question: 'How do I find the square footage of multiple rooms?', answer: 'For identical areas, enter one and set the quantity. For rooms of different sizes, calculate each separately and add the totals — that combined figure is what you order flooring or paint against.' },
  { question: 'How much flooring or paint do I need?', answer: 'Take your total square footage, add 5–10% for waste and cuts, then divide by the coverage per box or per gallon on the product label. Enter a price to get a cost estimate, priced per square foot, square yard, square metre or acre as your supplier quotes it.' },
  { question: 'How many square feet are in a square yard or an acre?', answer: 'One square yard equals 9 square feet — carpet is often priced per square yard — and one acre equals 43,560 square feet. One square metre is about 10.7639 square feet. Every result below is shown in all of these units.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" rail intro="Calculate the square footage of any area — rectangle, border, circle, ring, triangle, trapezoid, sector or parallelogram — with per-measurement units and an optional cost estimate." faqs={faqs} reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-29' }}>
  <SquareFootageCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use these calculators</h2>
    <p>
      Each shape above is its own calculator, so an answer stays on screen while you work out the
      next one — useful when a job is a patio plus the path around it, or a floor plus a bay window.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick the shape that matches the area you are measuring.</li>
      <li>Enter each <strong>measurement</strong> and choose the unit beside it. Every measurement has its own unit, so a length in feet and a width in inches are fine together.</li>
      <li>Set the <strong>quantity</strong> if you have several identical areas, and optionally a <strong>price</strong> per square foot, square yard, square metre or acre.</li>
      <li>Press that shape's <strong>Calculate</strong> button. The result is in square feet, with the same area in square inches, square yards, square metres and acres beneath it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The nine formulas</h2>
    <p>Every measurement is converted to feet first, then the shape's formula applies:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rectangle</strong> — length × width.</li>
      <li><strong>Rectangle Border</strong> — the outer rectangle less the rectangle left inside the border, which sits <em>inside</em> the dimensions you enter: (l × w) − (l − 2b) × (w − 2b).</li>
      <li><strong>Circle</strong> — π × (diameter ÷ 2)².</li>
      <li><strong>Ring</strong> — the outer disc less the disc inside the border: π × (r² − (r − b)²).</li>
      <li><strong>Triangle with Edge Lengths</strong> — Heron's formula from the three sides, with s = (a + b + c) ÷ 2: √(s(s − a)(s − b)(s − c)).</li>
      <li><strong>Triangle with Base &amp; Height</strong> — (base × height) ÷ 2.</li>
      <li><strong>Trapezoid</strong> — ((base 1 + base 2) ÷ 2) × height.</li>
      <li><strong>Sector</strong> — (angle in radians ÷ 2) × radius², so a 90° wedge is a quarter of the circle.</li>
      <li><strong>Parallelogram</strong> — base × perpendicular height, not the slanted side.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A bedroom 12 ft long and 15 ft wide is 12 × 15 = <strong>180 square feet</strong>, which is
      about <strong>16.7225 square metres</strong> or exactly <strong>20 square yards</strong>. Set the
      quantity to 2 for a pair of identical rooms and the total becomes 360 square feet; enter a
      flooring price of <strong>$3.50</strong> per square foot and the estimate is 360 × 3.50 =
      <strong>$1,260</strong> before any waste allowance.
    </p>

    <h2>Square footage conversions</h2>
    <p>Different materials are sold in different units, so it helps to know the equivalents:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Unit</th><th>In square feet</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1 square inch</td><td>0.00694 sq ft</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 square foot</td><td>1 sq ft</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 square yard</td><td>9 sq ft</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 square metre</td><td>10.7639 sq ft</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 acre</td><td>43,560 sq ft</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      These follow from one definition: a foot is exactly 0.3048 metres. That is where 10.7639 comes
      from, and why the figures above are computed from it rather than from a rounded factor.
    </p>

    <h2>Measuring a room accurately</h2>
    <p>Run a tape measure or laser measure along the longest wall for the length and the widest wall for the width, at floor level. Round to the nearest few inches — flooring and paint are estimates, not engineering. Measure into doorways and closets only if you intend to floor them. A quick sketch with each wall's length noted saves re-measuring later.</p>

    <h2>L-shaped and irregular rooms</h2>
    <p>Any awkward shape becomes easy once you break it into pieces:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Divide the floor plan into rectangles, triangles and circles.</li>
      <li>Calculate each piece with the matching calculator above.</li>
      <li>Add the totals together.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>An L-shaped living room is just two rectangles — say a 12 × 15 main area (180 sq ft) plus a 6 × 8 alcove (48 sq ft) for 228 sq ft in total. A room with a rounded bay is a rectangle plus a sector.</p>

    <h2>Estimating materials and cost</h2>
    <p>For flooring, tile or paint, take your total square footage and add about 5–10% for waste, offcuts and mistakes, then enter a price for a cost estimate. Price in whatever unit your supplier quotes — carpet by the square yard, tile by the square metre, land by the acre — and the conversion is handled for you. For a slab you're pouring rather than a floor you're covering, the <a href="/everyday/concrete-calculator">concrete calculator</a> works in cubic yards and bags.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
